    "summary": "Dark Star marathon, soaring Bertha>Greatest Story, tight jams",
    "blurb": "The first set shines with a gorgeous Bertha > Greatest Story Ever Told and a seamless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider. The second set delivers a monster Dark Star that flows into Mind Left Body Jam, Eyes Of The World and an emotional China Doll. Jerry’s melodic guitar work and Phil’s wicked bass solo anchor the high‑energy performance.",
    "review": "Winterland, 11 Nov 1973 – this night is often cited as one of the band’s finest late‑'70s outings. A bright, high‑octane first set kicks off with The Promised Land before launching into a gorgeous Bertha > Greatest Story Ever Told that many reviewers call \"gorgeous and high‑energy\". The China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue glides smoothly, setting up a mellow groove that carries through Loose Lucy and the Weather Report Suite into a soaring Let It Grow.  \nThe second set is where the show truly ascends: a monster Dark Star (35 min) erupts into an inspired Mind Left Body Jam, then rolls into Eyes Of The World before resolving in a heartfelt China Doll. Jerry’s guitar soars—his solo on Dark Star described as \"angelic\" and \"thinking‑man's\"—while Phil delivers a \"wicked\" bass launch that fuels the jam. Keith’s piano adds jazzy color, especially on El Paso and throughout the improvisations, and Bill Kreutzmann’s tight pocket keeps everything grounded. The encore closes with Uncle John's Band, a raucous Johnny B. Goode jump‑in, and the classic Good Night farewell. Across all available recordings the performance feels locked in, making this Winterland show a must‑listen for anyone chasing that 1973‑74 magic.",
